 .SH NAME
 tailcall \- Replace the current procedure with another command
 .SH SYNOPSIS
-\fBtailcall \fIcommand\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?
+\fBtailcall \fR?\fIcommand\fR? ?\fIarg ...\fR?
 .BE
 .SH DESCRIPTION
 .PP
 The \fBtailcall\fR command replaces the currently executing procedure, lambda
 application, or method with another command. The \fIcommand\fR, which will
@@ -24,12 +24,19 @@
 .PP
 .CS
 return [uplevel 1 [list \fIcommand\fR ?\fIarg ...\fR?]]
 .CE
 .PP
-This command may not be invoked from within an \fBuplevel\fR into a procedure
-or inside a \fBcatch\fR inside a procedure or lambda.
+This command may not be invoked from within an \fBuplevel\fR into a procedure.
+.PP
+\fBtailcall\fR schedules \fIcommand\fR and then completes with the return
+code \fBTCL_RETURN\fR, like \fBreturn\fR. If that code is caught (e.g., by
+\fBcatch\fR or \fBtry\fR) the procedure keeps running, but \fIcommand\fR is
+still invoked in its place when it eventually returns, and the result of
+\fIcommand\fR becomes the result of the procedure. A later \fBtailcall\fR
+replaces a pending one; \fBtailcall\fR without arguments cancels a pending
+tailcall and returns an empty result.
